    },
    {
      "Necessity of the interference in a democratic society": "For an interference with freedom of expression to be considered ‘necessary in a democratic society’, it must correspond to a pressing social need and be proportionate to the legitimate aim pursued. In the present case, the national authorities based the applicant’s conviction and pre-trial detention on her political speeches, participation in meetings and interviews, without demonstrating that her expressions amounted to a call for violence, armed resistance or hatred. The indictment and court decisions relied heavily on the applicant’s political activities, such as her speeches advocating for democratic solutions and her leadership in the DTK, which she consistently described as a civil society platform (see paragraphs 21, 28 and 29). The Court has held that the mere fact that expressions may be perceived as controversial, shock or disturb cannot justify restrictions on freedom of expression under Article 10 (see no. 67360/11)."
